drills.solution:
export function createLineSplitter() {
  let tail = '';
  const strip = (line) => (line.endsWith('\r') ? line.slice(0, -1) : line);
  return {
    push(chunk) {
      const parts = (tail + chunk).split('\n');
      tail = parts.pop();
      return parts.map(strip);
    },
    end() {
      const rest = tail;
      tail = '';
      return rest === '' ? [] : [strip(rest)];
    },
  };
}

tests.code:
assert.equal(typeof mod.createLineSplitter, 'function', 'export createLineSplitter');
const s = mod.createLineSplitter();
assert.deepEqual(s.push('ab'), []);
assert.deepEqual(s.push('c\nde'), ['abc']);
assert.deepEqual(s.end(), ['de']);

const s = mod.createLineSplitter();
assert.deepEqual(s.push('a\r\nb\r'), ['a']);
assert.deepEqual(s.push('\nc'), ['b']);
assert.deepEqual(s.end(), ['c']);

const a = mod.createLineSplitter();
const b = mod.createLineSplitter();
a.push('left');
assert.deepEqual(b.push('right\n'), ['right']);
assert.deepEqual(a.end(), ['left']);

drills.explanation:
Prepend the stored tail, split, and keep the LAST piece (it has no newline yet) for next time:

```js
push(chunk) {
  const parts = (tail + chunk).split('\n');
  tail = parts.pop();
  return parts.map(strip);
}
```

Stripping `\r` only when a line is completed is what handles a `\r` and `\n` arriving in different chunks: the `\r` waits in the tail until its `\n` arrives.

Three plausible slips. Normalising each chunk with `chunk.replace(/\r\n/g, '\n')` instead of stripping completed lines misses a `\r` that ends one chunk while its `\n` starts the next, so "CRLF is removed, even split between chunks" fails. Dropping empty strings with `.filter(Boolean)` also drops real blank lines, and "several lines in one chunk, empty lines kept" fails. An `end()` that always returns `[tail]` fails "nothing pushed means nothing at the end (boundary)".

